+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+:func:`rich.progress.open` reads a file and shows progress while its bytes are consumed. Pass a path, and the total defaults to the size of the file in bytes. The default mode is text (``'r'``), so ``read()`` returns a ``str``; use ``'rb'`` to get ``bytes`` instead::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with rich.progress.open("data.json") as text_file:
+        contents = text_file.read()
+
+    with rich.progress.open("data.bin", "rb") as binary_file:
+        contents = binary_file.read()
+
+The file is closed when the ``with`` block exits.
+
+If you already have a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, :meth:`rich.progress.Progress.open` adds the file as a task on it instead. It takes the same path and mode arguments, but it doesn't start the display, and you use the returned file in a ``with`` block of your own.
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+:func:`rich.progress.wrap_file` shows progress while reading an already-open binary file-like object. Only binary files are supported, and you must give the total number of bytes. The wrapped file is not closed when the ``with`` block exits, so the caller still owns it::
+
+    import io
+    import rich.progress
+
+    source = io.BytesIO(b"some bytes to read")
+    with rich.progress.wrap_file(source, total=len(source.getvalue())) as reader:
+        data = reader.read()
+    source.close()
+
+If you already have a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, :meth:`rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` attaches a reader to one of its tasks instead. In that case the total can be omitted when the task already has one.
+
 Multiple Progress
 -----------------
 
